.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE_STREAM 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize_stream \- serialize a list of documents as a multi-document YAML stream
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize_stream(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Write a list of documents back as a YAML stream.  This is the
counterpart of parsing a stream: cyaml_parse(3) gives a CYAML_LIST with
one element for each document of an input that holds more than one
document, and this function writes each element of such a list as a
document of its own.  Each document starts with a "---" line, followed
by the block-style YAML that cyaml_serialize(3) writes for the element,
so parsing the result of a list of two or more documents gives back an
equal list.  A stream of Kubernetes manifests, for example, can be
parsed, edited and written back for a tool that reads it one document
at a time.

An empty list gives an empty stream, the empty string "".  Parsing an
empty string gives a CYAML_NULL and not an empty list, because a stream
with no documents and an empty document read the same.

A node that is not a list, a NULL node included, is written as a
stream of one document, so that the root of a one-document parse
writes back as that same document.  A list that is the root of a
single document cannot be told apart from a stream of documents, in
the parse or here, so write such a document with cyaml_serialize(3).

A tag of the list itself is not written, because a stream has no node
that could carry it; a list that a parse of a stream gives never
carries one.

Each document stands alone, and the output holds no directive and no
anchor: a tag goes out in the same form as in cyaml_serialize(3), which
needs no %TAG directive, and an alias of the parsed input is already an
independent copy in the tree.  Scalars, dictionary keys, floats, the
member order of a dictionary and the locale all follow the rules of
cyaml_serialize(3), and each document obeys the same 500-level nesting
limit.

The returned string comes from the allocator of node.  Free it with
cyaml_serialize_free() when the tree used the default allocator, and
with cyaml_serialize_free_mp() and that same allocator when the tree
used a custom one.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A null-terminated string on the heap on success, or NULL when the
function runs out of memory or when a document is nested more than 500
levels deep.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
char *err = NULL;
cyaml docs = cyaml_parse(stream, &err);   /* three manifests */
if (!docs) {
    fprintf(stderr, "parse failed: %s\en", err);
    return -1;
}
cyaml_set(docs, "#1.spec.replicas", 5);
char *out = cyaml_serialize_stream(docs);
if (out)   /* "---\enapiVersion: v1\en...\en---\enapiVersion: apps/v1\en..." */
    fputs(out, stdout);
cyaml_serialize_free(out);
cyaml_destroy(docs);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_parse (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_flow (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free_mp (3)
